    "\n",
    "With $m = 1$ the process is subcritical for every $\\rho$, so sections 3.7a–d cannot distinguish a correct theory from a trivial one. To reach $\\rho\\kappa > 1$ inside $[0,1]$ we raise the number of attempts per fired trap to $m = 5$ and make every attempt long-range ($p_{\\text{long}} = 1$) with a distance-independent success probability of 0.9. Short-range attempts are no use here: they can only reach the few cells next to the firing trap, most of which are already spent. In the mixture form this gives $\\kappa = m\\,p_{\\text{long}}\\,E[p_{\\text{succ,long}}(k)] = 5 \\times 0.9 = 4.5$ and $\\rho_{\\text{onset}} = 1/\\kappa \\approx 0.22$.\n",
